The Tithonian (Late Jurassic) represents a high point in species diversity of ichthyosaurs, a successful group of Mesozoic marine reptiles; however, variation in diet and body shape is not correspondingly high. Numerous exceptional fossil deposits from a range of palaeolatitudes in both the Northern and Southern Hemispheres are thought to be responsible for the high observed number of species during this time interval; however, provincialism has also been proposed as a driver. Here, we re-evaluate the ichthyosaur fauna of the German lithographic limestones and provide a revised estimate for species diversity from these areas of exceptional preservation. Unlike coeval fossil localities, only a single accepted ichthyosaurian genus from the Late Jurassic of Germany, Aegirosaurus leptospondylus, has been described to date, suggesting sampling biases. Here, we describe a second genus from these deposits, Jabalisaurus tethyensis sp. nov., based on fossils previously included within Aegirosaurus. New observations provide additional details on the skeletal anatomy, fossilized soft tissues such as skin, and diet in J. tethyensis. Jabalisaurus was previously known only from the Late Jurassic of Mexico. The presence of this genus in Europe leads us to reject the regional provincialism hypothesis and suggests instead the existence of a unique Peri-Tethyan ichthyosaurian fauna, distinct from both the Boreal/sub-Boreal and Eastern Pacific faunas and potentially controlled by climate parameters such as temperature.
